<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mixtecs</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ñuu Savi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mixtecs, who call themselves Ñuu Savi, 'people of the rain', are an indigenous people of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a> and neighbouring parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> and Puebla in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. Before the Spanish conquest they lived in many small kingdoms such as Tilantongo and Tututepec, whose histories they recorded in painted screenfold books, and were known for gold work and turquoise mosaic. Many fell under Aztec rule in the 15th century. Mixtec languages are still widely spoken.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mixtecs.jpg" alt="Mixtecs" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: [2] · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jarabe_Mixteco.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Mixtec comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> word mixtecah, meaning cloud people. The Mixtecs call themselves by several names, such as ñuù savi, all of which can be translated as the land of the rain. Their historic homeland, La Mixteca, covers roughly the western half of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a> and parts of Puebla and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a>. It is divided into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixteca-alta">Mixteca Alta</a>, the highlands, the drier Mixteca Baja and the humid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixteca-costa">Mixteca Costa</a> on the Pacific coast.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixteca-alta">Mixteca Alta</a> has been studied most by archaeologists, with evidence of settlement from the Archaic and Early Formative periods, and the first urbanised sites emerged there. For most of Mixtec history it was the dominant political force, with the capitals in the central highlands. The Mixtec culture is held to have lasted from around 1500 BCE until the Spanish conquest. Before it, the Mixtecs numbered around 1.5 million.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Mixtec kingdoms competed and allied with each other and with Zapotec kingdoms in the Central Valleys, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-oaxaca">Valley of Oaxaca</a> was often a disputed border region. Important centres included Tilantongo, Achiutla, Cuilapan, Tlaxiaco and Tututepec, and the Mixtecs also made major constructions at Monte Albán, a city of Zapotec origin that they came to control. The Costa came under Mixtec control through the campaigns of the cultural hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-deer-jaguar-claw">Eight Deer Jaguar Claw</a>, who set out from Tilantongo, conquered kingdoms on the way and established the capital of Tututepec in the lower Río Verde valley.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> moved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a> in the 1450s and defeated the Mixtecs in 1458. In 1486 they founded a fort on the hill of Huaxyácac, now El Fortín, which let them collect tribute from Mixtecs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zapotecs">Zapotecs</a>, though not every Mixtec town became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. The Mixtecs were later subdued by the Spanish and their central Mexican allies in the 16th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Mixtecs wrote their histories and genealogies in painted fold-books on deerskin. The best-known story is that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-deer-jaguar-claw">Lord Eight Deer</a>, told in several codices including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-bodley">Codex Bodley</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-zouche-nuttall">Codex Zouche-Nuttall</a>. They were goldsmiths said to be the foremost of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, practising lost-wax casting, and their gold work formed part of the tribute they paid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>. Turquoise mosaic masks were given as gifts to seal alliances, worn by those impersonating a god, and fixed to funerary bundles seen as oracles.</p><p>The Mixtec languages form a major branch of the Oto-Manguean family. About 800,000 Mixtec people live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> today, and many have migrated within Mexico and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mixtec” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The law distinguished a republic of Spaniards from a republic of Indians, whose towns kept their own councils and nobles, and Nahuatl, <b>Mixtec</b> and other languages were used in local records.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Francisco de Orozco arrived in 1521; the Zapotec and <b>Mixtecs</b> soon submitted, though Antequera resisted until the end of that year.</blockquote><a href="/culture/zapotec-civilisation">Zapotec civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Coixtlahuaca developed in a valley of the Mixteca Alta inhabited by Chocho (Ngiwa) and <b>Mixtec</b> people.</blockquote><a href="/state/coixtlahuaca">Coixtlahuaca</a></li><li><blockquote>Mitla served as a religious centre and was linked politically with the Zapotec kingdom of Zaachila, while sharing <b>Mixtec</b> artistic influences.</blockquote><a href="/state/mitla">Mitla</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mixtec</b> nobles later reused its tombs, including the famous Tomb 7 treasure.</blockquote><a href="/state/monte-alban">Monte Albán</a></li><li><blockquote>Teozacoalco, Chiyo Cahnu in <b>Mixtec</b>, was a royal seat in the western Mixteca Alta by about 1000.</blockquote><a href="/state/teozacoalco">Teozacoalco</a></li></ul></section>
